- Welcome to Virgulas
  A local-first outliner for thinking in connected, expandable notes. This document is a guided tour: edit it freely, then choose storage when you are ready to keep your work. Sections marked ▶ hold the details — expand the ones you need.
+ Start writing
  Select a node to edit its title. Press `Escape` to leave editing. Press `Enter` to add the next node; when the current node has visible children, `Enter` adds the first child.
  + Editing details
    - `Shift+Enter` switches between a title and its optional **Description**. Empty descriptions stay out of the way, and longer ones preview while you browse.
    - Use **bold**, __italic__, `code`, [links](https://virgulas.com), and images in a title or description. Markdown renders while browsing and returns to plain text while editing.
    - Type `#project` or `@name` in any note. Select the rendered pill to search for that #tag or @mention.
    - Paste a single line normally. Paste multiple lines, or text beginning with `-` or `+`, to turn a VMD outline fragment into nodes.
- Shape the outline
  Use `Tab` and `Shift+Tab` to indent or outdent the selected node. On mobile, swipe right or left instead. Use `Alt+↑` and `Alt+↓` to move a node among its siblings.
  + Structure details
    - Select neighboring siblings with `Shift+↑` or `Shift+↓`, then move, indent, outdent, collapse, or delete the group together.
    - Press `Ctrl+Space`, or use the ▶/▼ control, to collapse and expand children. A collapsed node with hidden children shows a hollow bullet.
    - Press `Ctrl+Backspace` to delete a node. Backspace on an empty title also deletes it; deleting a node with children asks for confirmation.
    - On desktop, `Ctrl+C` with a title focused and no text selected copies that node and its descendants as VMD.
  + A collapsed example
    - Child notes stay here until you expand this node.
    - Collapsing is useful for keeping a long plan calm and scannable.
- Navigate and find
  Select any bullet to zoom into that node. Its children become your working view; use the breadcrumb to go back. On desktop, `Alt+→` zooms in and `Alt+←` zooms out. The zoomed node is recorded in the URL, so a zoom can be reloaded or shared.
  + Search details
    - Press `Escape` with no note focused to search on desktop; on mobile use the Search button in the status bar.
    - Matching text is highlighted and non-matches are hidden. Lowercase queries are case-insensitive; any uppercase letter makes the search case-sensitive.
    - Use `Tab` and `Shift+Tab` (or `↑` and `↓`) to move through results, then `Enter` to open one. Opening a result zooms to its nearest collapsed ancestor. Press `Escape` again to close search.
- Turn notes into tasks
  Press `Ctrl+Enter` to cycle a note through plain, pending, and done. You can also begin its title with `[ ] ` or `[x] `. Click the checkbox to toggle it.
  - [ ] Plan the next review due:2026-09-15
    This task is overdue, so it is highlighted in the task list. Due dates render as chips and are written as `due:yyyy-MM-dd`.
  - [ ] Pay rent due:2026-09-28 rec:1m
    `rec:1m` repeats this task monthly. Completing a recurring task advances its due date instead of marking it done.
  + Task list details
    - Open Tasks with `Ctrl+Alt+K` or the checklist button in the status bar. It groups work into Pending, Scheduled, and Done for your current zoom.
    - Select a row to jump to that task; the checkbox in the row toggles it without navigating.
    - Scheduled tasks can be filtered to a 3, 7, or 30 day window, or All, to hide distant recurring work.
- Capture on the go
  Virgulas can collect text without opening the app, and file it into an Inbox node later.
  + Capture details
    - Long-press the installed app icon and choose **Quick capture**, or share text to Virgulas from another app on Android.
    - Open `https://virgulas.com/?quick-add=buy%20milk` from an automation tool such as Tasker.
    - Copy the **Save to** bookmarklet from Options → Quick capture and click it on any page.
    - A capture visit stays locked: no passphrase, no decryption, no network. Captures wait in a device-local queue and are filed into your Inbox node on the next unlock.
    - A shared page becomes a `[title](url)` link, and highlighted text becomes its description.
- Keep your work
  This tour is in memory only and disappears when the tab closes. Select **Enable Secure Storage** above to choose a persistent mode.
  + Storage modes
    - **Memory** keeps the document in this tab only. It is the default until you choose something else, and the app remembers that choice.
    - **Local** encrypts this browser's document with a passphrase you choose. The passphrase cannot be recovered.
    - **Remote** signs in with an account email and password, then encrypts and syncs your document with a separate passphrase. Other devices need both the account and the passphrase.
    - **File** opens or creates a plain `.vmd` file on your device. Changes are saved back to the file after you pause typing.
  + Encryption and backups
    - Local and Remote documents are encrypted with AES-GCM-256 under a PBKDF2 key (600,000 iterations, per-document salt). Only the salt and ciphertext leave the device.
    - In Local or Remote mode, Options → Data lets you export or import a `.vmd` backup. Importing replaces the document and re-encrypts it on the next save.
    - Options also lets you change your encryption passphrase and enable an optional biometric prompt on this device.
    - Remote mode adds account controls, including Delete account, which removes the encrypted document stored on the server and signs you out here.
- Make it yours
  Select **Options** in the status bar to change the theme, manage the current storage session, export a backup, or open the source repository. The current mode and sync state are also shown there.
  + Interface details
    - On desktop, `Ctrl+Alt+T` toggles the theme and `Ctrl+Alt+W` toggles wide mode.
    - The status bar shows the storage mode, the sync indicator when signed in, Tasks, Search on mobile, and the **?** button for the complete keyboard shortcut reference on desktop.
    - Virgulas follows `prefers-reduced-motion` and shows visible focus rings for keyboard navigation.
